        {
          "schema_version": 2,
          "ts": "2026-10-14T01:19:27.479107Z",
          "trace_id": "bbe8393e918e4b01b90dfcf5de1f85ba",
          "session_id": "full-mt-m-004",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Should I be on something else as well?",
          "answer": "I need a bit more information before I can answer well. Could you tell me:\n\n1. **Which condition** you mean. Is it asthma, or something else?\n2. **What you currently take**, including any daily inhalers, tablets or other treatments. You can list them by the name on the box or by what they are for.\n\nIn general terms, a clinician decides on extra treatment by reviewing how well the condition is controlled and what has happened recently. For asthma, the guideline *Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy* says treatment is arranged in steps. The step is chosen from current symptom control and exacerbation history (flare-ups that need extra treatment or medical care), and it is reviewed over time rather than fixed. The same guideline says that treating asthma with a reliever alone is no longer considered appropriate, because the underlying inflammation is left untreated. If you have asthma and rely only on a reliever, that is worth raising with your prescriber.\n\nFor other conditions, the sources I can search differ. For example, the COPD guidance describes choosing treatment by symptom burden and flare-up history, and the anxiety and cholesterol guidance describes reviewing response at set intervals before adding or switching treatment. I cannot tell you which of these applies to you without knowing your condition.\n\nPlease take this question to your prescriber or pharmacist. They can check your full medicine list, your symptoms and your history, and tell you whether an additional medicine is appropriate. Do not start, stop or change any medicine based on this general information.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
